The study of $	au$-lepton semileptonic three-body decays offers an exciting opportunity to gain insight into CP symmetry breaking and test the Standard Model's predictions. This paper provides a comprehensive analysis of the kinematic and angular properties of these decays, examining the impact of CP violation on various distributions. Focusing on specific decay processes, including $	au^{\pm}	o\pi^{\pm}\ell^{\mp}
u_{\ell}$ and $	au^{\pm}	o K^{\pm}\ell^{\mp}
u_{\ell}$, we find that certain kinematic variables can effectively isolate CP symmetry violations. Additionally, we investigate the $	au^{\pm}	o K^{\pm}\ell^{\mp}
u_{\ell}$ decay, suggesting that the KEDR experiment at VEPP-4M can achieve a 10$^{-4}$ level of CP asymmetry measurement using the $	au^{\pm}	o\pi^{\pm}e^{\mp}
u_{e}$ decay. Furthermore, we propose that $	au$-lepton pair production at a future linear $e^{+}e^{-}$ collider could enable the measurement of CP asymmetries at a 10$^{-5}$ precision.
